reviews.summary:
A hostname-parsed protocol table guards every model backend at the wire. host_mandated_protocol (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:112) takes the hostname from hostname_of (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:85). hostname_of parses strings with httpx.URL, the parser the wire dials with (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:103). A fullwidth, ideographic or halfwidth-ideographic dot spelling of a vendor host is therefore refused at HybridRouter.detect() as well as at the wire. urlsplit remains the fallback for text httpx rejects, and httpx cannot dial that text either. The host is lower-cased with the trailing dot dropped, then matched exactly or as whole labels (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:62-69): api.anthropic.com -> anthropic_messages; api.openai.com and one regional label -> openai; bedrock-runtime[-fips].<region>.amazonaws.com[.cn] -> bedrock_converse. Lookalike suffixes, path spoofs and userinfo spoofs mandate nothing. Each backend's ledger name declares its protocol (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:49). Every llm_async_client caller on this head is declared: anthropic, gemini (gemini_cache included), openai-responses, xai, openrouter, lm-studio, ollama and vlm. protocol_refusal (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:131) also refuses an undeclared backend on a mandated host. The check lives in the egress-ledger request hook that every backend's llm_async_client carries (agents/core/llm/egress.py:67-70), so it runs on every redirect hop. A mismatch records an allowed=False ledger row with the reason and raises before the transport is touched, so neither the body nor the auth header leaves. HybridRouter.detect refuses a compatible base URL on a host that mandates another protocol (agents/core/llm/hybrid_router.py:375); the refusal is logged and the backend is not built. HostProtocolRefused subclasses Exception, neither OSError nor httpx.HTTPError (agents/core/llm/host_protocol.py:73), so no connection-failure handler reads a refusal as 'backend down'. OllamaBackend.resolve_context_window logs a warning and returns unknown on a refusal (agents/core/llm/base.py:720), instead of raising out of compaction and route planning. Adaptation from Hermes: Nerva has no per-session api_mode that can go stale, so the mandate refuses a mismatched backend rather than re-picking a protocol. Nerva's OpenAI family admits both OpenAI dialects (chat completions and Responses) at api.openai.com. Base URLs remain env-only until the named-custom-endpoints row exists, and the wire guard covers any base URL unchanged. Coverage is tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py (43 tests):
- vendor hosts, including the 5 IDNA-dot spellings (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:61);
- 15 lookalike, path, userinfo and other hosts (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:82);
- an OpenAI-protocol backend aimed at api.anthropic.com sends zero requests and leaves two blocked ledger rows (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:119);
- the client raises before dispatch (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:141);
- matching and unmandated hosts still flow (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:151);
- a redirect onto a vendor host is refused per hop (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:164);
- router refusals, including both IDNA spellings (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:204, tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:213, tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:234);
- the default and custom endpoints are still built (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:222);
- the refusal is neither an OSError nor an httpx.HTTPError (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:247);
- the Ollama probe degrades and leaves a blocked ledger row (tests/test_host_mandated_protocol.py:254).
All wire tests use the real client, with httpx.MockTransport injected through llm_async_client's public transport kwarg.

Orchestrator._recall_block (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2774) gates and bounds the pre-turn recall, skips it while an earlier one is stuck, and warms the next turn, the way Hermes' memory prefetch does. It is reached from the streaming and parallel turn paths. The 2026-09-24 review rounds fixed what had made the bound hollow, built the warm-up half, which had been missing, and made a delete reach every cached result and the store itself.

(1) Triviality gate. is_trivial_prompt (agents/core/memory/recall_gate.py:67) returns true for:
- empty input;
- a command-shaped slash text (agents/core/memory/recall_gate.py:49). Nerva dispatches commands before recall, so a path such as "/etc/hosts changed, why?" is a question;
- a bare acknowledgement or greeting followed only by punctuation or emoji: the Hermes list plus Romanian (_ACKS, agents/core/memory/recall_gate.py:31), with internal commas read as spaces;
- punctuation alone ("?", "…?", "❓", "$?"), and an emoji-only reaction unless it comes with a question mark ("🦷?" is a question); only the "other symbol" category counts as an emoji (agents/core/memory/recall_gate.py:80).
Text is compared after NFC normalisation with format characters removed (agents/core/memory/recall_gate.py:61). k8s, yolo, "ok, and what about X?" and any non-command text over 40 characters are never trivial. _recall_block returns '' before any embedding (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2790).

(2) Job pins. A job-model-pinned turn never recalls, and the check runs before any handoff or warm context (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2794).

(3) Hard timeout. _bounded_recall_hits (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2693) awaits _recall_hits (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2577) for at most memory.recall_timeout_s. _recall_hits covers the optional H433 rewrite, the query embedding and the fused vector ⊕ graph search.
- The setting is seeded at 8 s like Hermes' _EXTERNAL_PREFETCH_TIMEOUT_S (agents/core/settings_db.py:239).
- _recall_timeout_s (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2568) reads a non-number, bool, NaN, infinity or non-positive value as 8 and clamps the rest to 0.1–60 s. The settings write path refuses a non-finite number for every number setting (agents/core/settings_db.py:678).
- On expiry it logs a WARNING (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2730).
- The living-memory rerank runs after the bound, off the event loop, only on the hits a turn freshly recalled (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2819); warm hits served again are not reranked again.

The bound holds for the turn, not only for the await:
- MemoryManager keeps a store lock for the vector store and the knowledge graph, separate from the conversation lock (agents/core/memory/manager.py:45-46; store_embedding at agents/core/memory/manager.py:266, hybrid_search at agents/core/memory/manager.py:293; a single vector remove at agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:327). A search stuck on Qdrant or Neo4j does not block saving the reply or any other session's turn.
- With MEMORY_EMBED_TURNS on, turn embeddings are written in the background, one at a time and oldest first, as Hermes' sync_all never writes a provider inline (_queue_turn_embedding, agents/core/memory/manager.py:146). Shutdown gives them 5 s to land and then drops what is still queued (agents/core/orchestrator.py:1339, agents/core/orchestrator.py:1343). The CLI REPL reads each line on a daemon thread, so the event loop and the queue keep running while the owner types, and it gives the queue 30 s to land on exit (_prompt, agents/run.py:52; agents/run.py:49).

(4) Skip while stuck. A timed-out recall cannot be killed, because its embedding and search run on worker threads. It is kept as a straggler, and so is a recall whose turn was cancelled; none is overwritten or cancelled under its threads (_adopt_recall_straggler, agents/core/orchestrator.py:2738). While any straggler runs, later recalls are skipped with an INFO line (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2710), as Hermes skips a provider whose previous prefetch is still alive.

(5) Next-turn warm-up. The hits a turn recalled, an empty result included, become its session's warm context (_keep_warm_recall, agents/core/orchestrator.py:2667, kept at agents/core/orchestrator.py:2820, capped at 256 sessions). When the next turn's own recall is unavailable (timed out, or skipped behind a stuck one), that warm context stands in (_warm_recall, agents/core/orchestrator.py:2681; agents/core/orchestrator.py:2813), with an INFO line naming it. It is never served:
- to another session;
- to a caller outside any turn (the autonomy worker, the nightly reflection, /api/context/compress), which neither keeps, serves nor consumes turn state, and whose stragglers leave no handoff (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2655). Work delegated inside a turn shares that turn's session;
- after a purge or a single-memory delete;
- past 10 minutes;
- to a trivial or pinned turn;
- when the new turn's recall ran and found nothing: that empty answer replaces it.

This is Hermes' queue_prefetch_all adapted to one retrieval memory:
- Hermes computes a background result from the finished turn's message and injects it next turn beside the new base context (Honcho's pending dialectic).
- Nerva's recall of that same message already ran at the turn's start, so it reuses those hits instead of a second round-trip.
- It uses them only when the new turn's own, fresher recall is unavailable, which is when a warmed result saves the turn.

(6) Same-question handoff, a Nerva extension. Hermes throws a timed-out result away. Here a straggler that finishes cleanly leaves its hits for one handoff, kept per session: the session's next recall takes them, serving them only for a retry of the same question within 10 minutes of completion (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2715). Another session never receives them.

(7) Deletes. Every recall reads the recall generation when it starts (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2804); its result is served, handed off or kept warm only while that generation is still current. A result a delete overtook is discarded when the search returns (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2732), before the rerank can reinforce it, or after the rerank (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2821), and nothing is kept under a stale generation.
- A purge ("forget me", data_purge.clear_live_memory) drops queued turn embeddings and bumps the generation, clearing every handoff and warm context (_forget_in_flight, agents/core/data_purge.py:570; _recall_purged, agents/core/orchestrator.py:2757). It does so before the wipe and again after it, the second time in a finally (agents/core/data_purge.py:454, agents/core/data_purge.py:458). In between, recall serves nothing at all (agents/core/orchestrator.py:2800), so pre-wipe memory is never served during a purge either.
- The vector store and the graph are wiped under the store lock (agents/core/data_purge.py:480), so a turn embedding already writing lands before the wipe. Queued embeddings and remember() carry the generation they started under and re-check it under that lock (agents/core/memory/manager.py:274). A search stuck on a dead backend, or a write slower than the 10 s wait, holds the lock: the wipe then goes ahead without it and never releases a lock it did not take. A write that lands after such a wipe removes its own record while it still holds the lock, so no search sees it (agents/core/memory/manager.py:277), and the forget reports the busy lock in not_erased instead of claiming a clean wipe (AUDIT-2).
- A single delete does the same through _recall_forget (agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:336): DELETE /api/kg/entities/{name} and DELETE /api/kg/relations, also when the call is cancelled mid-delete (agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:696, agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:757); an entity edit, which replaces its properties (agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:670); and a consolidation apply that actually removed a vector, even one whose re-embedding then failed (agents/core/routers/memory_kg.py:434).
- Known limit: a turn already running when the purge starts still saves its reply afterwards; the purge does not quiesce turns.

